// Test: bugfixes/type_nullable_declaration
// Grammar: type = base_type , [ "?" ] ;  (foundation/spec/grammar/grammar.ebnf)
// Semantic: spec 04-type-system.md §"Nullable Types" — T? holds T or null;
//   `!` moves T? → T, trapping on null; `default` provides a fallback.
//
// Regression: NULLABLE-TYPE-SPEC-AND-IMPL #08af02a9. The lexer passthrough
//   added in 0.33.141 stopped LEX001 firing on `T?` but the parser had no
//   way to actually consume the `?` token, so `string? maybe = "hello"`
//   fell through to SEM007 "Undefined variable 'maybe'". This lifts the
//   feature end-to-end: real TokenKind::Question token, Type::Nullable(T)
//   AST variant, parse_type wraps on trailing `?`, HIR lowers to inner
//   representation (T? and T share WASM shape — pointers use 0 as null
//   sentinel, matching `!` (RequiredAssert)).
//
// Fixed in: compiler 0.33.145
//
// Expected output:
//   hello

start:
	string? maybe = "hello"
	if maybe != null
		print(maybe)
